#Objective
Secure the SSH service to :
•	limit unauthorized access,
•	reduce the risk of brute-force attacks,
•	improve remote server security.


#Context
The SSH protocol is :
•	essential,
•	but also a frequent target of attacks.
System administrators typically implement :
•	disabling root login,
•	disabling passwords,
•	limiting authorized users,
•	changing SSH configuration.


#SSH Security Strategy
| Setting                 | Value     |
| ----------------------- | --------- |
| Root Login              | Disabled  |
| Password Authentication | Disabled  |
| SSH Protocol            | Version 2 |
| Allowed Users           | gadmin    |


#IMPORTANT
BEFORE making any changes:
Create a VMware snapshot:
MODULE5_BEFORE_SECURITY_HARDENING
Using:
VMware Workstation


#Backup SSH Configuration
Save SSH configuration
sudo cp /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.bak
ls -l /etc/ssh/sshd_config*


#SSH Key Generation
Objective: Use SSH key authentication instead of passwords.
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096
ssh-copy-id gadmin@localhost
ssh gadmin@localhost


#SSH Configuration Hardening
sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config
PermitRootLogin no
PasswordAuthentication no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
Protocol 2
AllowUsers gadmin
MaxAuthTries 3
ClientAliveInterval 300
ClientAliveCountMax 2


#Explanations
| Directive                 | Purpose                 |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| PermitRootLogin no        | Block acces root        |
| PasswordAuthentication no | Disable password        |
| PubkeyAuthentication yes  | Activate SSH keys       |
| AllowUsers                | Restrict access         |
| MaxAuthTries 3            | Bruteforce limit        |


#Validate SSH Configuration
sudo sshd -t
sudo systemctl restart ssh
sudo systemctl status ssh --no-pager
ssh gadmin@localhost
